      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the ragu: heat the oil in a large pan. Cook the onion, carrot and celery for 10 minutes over medium-low heat. Add the garlic and cook for 1 more minute."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Increase the heat. Brown the beef mince in batches, then the pork. Do not stir too soon -- let colour build. Add the wine and scrape up any bits. Reduce by half."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the tinned tomatoes and bay leaves. Season. Simmer very gently for at least 60 minutes. The ragu should be thick. Set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the bechamel: melt the butter in a saucepan over medium heat. Add the flour and stir for 2 minutes to cook out the raw taste. Add the warm milk gradually, whisking constantly to avoid lumps. Cook for 5 to 8 minutes until thick. Season with salt and nutmeg."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oven to 180C. Spread a thin layer of bechamel in a large baking dish. Add a layer of lasagne sheets. Add a layer of ragu, then bechamel. Repeat until all the pasta and sauces are used. Finish with bechamel on top."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Scatter the Parmesan over the top. Bake for 40 to 45 minutes until golden and bubbling at the edges."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Rest for 15 minutes before cutting. This allows the layers to set and makes slicing much easier."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Lasagne is soggy",
              "cause": "Ragu was too wet or bechamel too thin",
              "fix": "Simmer the ragu longer next time. Make the bechamel slightly thicker than you think you need."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Dry and tight in the middle",
              "cause": "Not enough sauce between the layers",
              "fix": "Be more generous with both sauces next time. The dried pasta absorbs a lot of liquid as it bakes."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Top is burnt but centre is cold",
              "cause": "Oven too high or dish too deep",
              "fix": "Cover with foil for the first 30 minutes, then uncover for the last 15 to brown."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Lasagne al forno is from Emilia-Romagna. The classic version uses green pasta made with spinach. It is Sunday lunch food -- the kind that takes all morning and feeds everyone. This version uses dried sheets for ease."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
